## Vercel Speed Insights Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the dj-website frontend application.

### Changes Made

**1. Package Installation**
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0 using npm
- Updated `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the new package and its dependencies

**2. Code Integration**
- Modified `src/App.tsx` to import and use the SpeedInsights component
- Added `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/react'` at the top of the file
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component in the App component's return statement, placed next to the existing `<Analytics />` component

### Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart), I implemented the React/Vite-specific integration:

- Used the `@vercel/speed-insights/react` import path (framework-specific for React)
- Placed the component at the app root level for comprehensive performance tracking
- Maintained consistency with the existing analytics setup already present in the project

### Files Modified
- `src/App.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights import and component
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency information

### Verification
✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
✅ Linter passed with no errors (`npm run lint`)
✅ All changes follow existing code patterns and conventions
✅ Component placement matches the existing Analytics integration

### Next Steps
After deployment to Vercel:
1. The Speed Insights feature should be enabled in the Vercel dashboard
2. Once users visit the site, performance metrics will begin appearing in the Speed Insights section
3. The `/_vercel/speed-insights/*` routes will be automatically added by Vercel

No additional configuration is required - the implementation is ready for deployment.
